::JOUR_U06_PRE_Q01::[MLA.JOUR.ETH.01] Source: Unit 6 Pretest Scenario. A student newsroom is preparing a final journalism portfolio. The portfolio includes reporting notes, revised articles, media-literacy checks, multimedia elements, collaboration workflow, an online publication page, and an oral presentation outline. Some parts are complete, some need revision, some need evidence reasoning, and some need final publication checks. Question: Pretest Question 1: Which choice best supports final Journalism portfolio readiness? {
=A. Check reporting evidence, revision, media choices, multimedia sequence, collaboration, publication, and presentation before final submission#Correct. Step 1: connect the answer to the MLA journalism standard. Step 2: use the embedded source to identify the reporting, writing, media, publication, collaboration, presentation, or evidence issue. Step 3: choose the option that protects accuracy, audience trust, organization, and publication readiness.
~B. Submit because the folder contains many files#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source instead of relying on a general impression. Step 2: identify the exact evidence, audience, revision, media, presentation, or workflow requirement. Step 3: reject choices that skip verification, ignore audience needs, weaken cohesion, or treat completion as mastery.
~C. Use the most dramatic option without checking accuracy#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source instead of relying on a general impression. Step 2: identify the exact evidence, audience, revision, media, presentation, or workflow requirement. Step 3: reject choices that skip verification, ignore audience needs, weaken cohesion, or treat completion as mastery.
~D. Skip audience purpose because this is only a pretest#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source instead of relying on a general impression. Step 2: identify the exact evidence, audience, revision, media, presentation, or workflow requirement. Step 3: reject choices that skip verification, ignore audience needs, weaken cohesion, or treat completion as mastery.
}

